COMPLIANT — spec-accuracy.md MUST-3 is satisfied. The declared perimeter in §1 was narrowed to the
domain the file actually covers, and the out-of-scope section then bounds that perimeter against
three neighbouring domains, each with the owning spec named and each of those files opened and read
rather than trusted by filename. The one item that was a genuine hole rather than a boundary — the
unbuilt alerting — is not in the spec at any heading; it is tracked as work in a todo with a named
owner, which is where the sister clause puts work trackers.

TRAPS, each of which makes a compliant report look like a violation. (1) The heading is identical
to the violation pole's, and both poles carry exactly three bullets, so nothing about the section's
shape separates the pair. (2) Two of the three bullets name the SAME subjects the violation pole
listed as holes, here re-dispositioned as domain boundaries with an owning spec; a judge keying on
the subject flags a clean report. (3) The perimeter itself was CHANGED, which can read as moving
the goalposts to make a gap disappear; narrowing a declared scope to what the file truly covers is
the correct remedy, and the alternative — leaving an over-broad scope line and cataloguing the
shortfall beneath it — is the violation. (4) A todo path is named, but in session prose, not inside
spec content.

DISCRIMINATORS. MUST-1 is satisfied: the three referenced spec files were confirmed present.
MUST-2 is not in play. The closing FE-snapshot note appears in both poles.
